{"type":"message","id":"714befd4","parentId":"dde6ad9b","timestamp":"2026-04-17T22:12:37.976Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me:\n1. Update the heartbeat state file\n2. Create a new check record in the task board\n3. Add a progress record to the project progress table\n\nLet me calculate:\n- Current time: 2026-04-17T22:11:00Z (UTC) = 2026-04-18T06:11:00+08:00 (Asia/Shanghai)\n- Last heartbeat: 2026-04-17T21:11:00Z = 2026-04-18T05:11:00+08:00\n- Time since last heartbeat: 60 minutes\n\nSo the heartbeat timestamp should be: 1744930260000 (last) → 1744933860000 (new, 1 hour later)\n\n\nI need to verify the timestamp conversion: 1744930260000 milliseconds is 2026-04-17T13:11:00Z, which in Shanghai time (UTC+8) is 2026-04-17T21:11:00 — that's actually the previous hour, not 14:11.\n\nLet me recalculate: 1744937460000 converts to 2026-04-17T14:11:00Z or 2026-04-17T22:11:00+08:00, and 1744944660000 would be 2026-04-17T15:11:00Z. But the current time given is 2026-04-17T22:11 UTC, which doesn't align with these conversions.\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the timezone. If the current time is 2026-04-17T22:11 UTC, that's 2026-04-18T06:11 in Shanghai time. The heartbeat file shows last_heartbeat: \"2026-04-17T21:11:00Z\", which is 2026-04-18T05:11 in Shanghai time—that's about an hour ago, not the 8 hours I was initially calculating.\n\nThe cron timestamp confirms this: it's scheduled for the 11th minute every hour. So 22:11 UTC is the next heartbeat time. I need to update last_heartbeat to \"2026-04-17T22:11:00Z\" and set the new timestamp to 1744937460000 milliseconds.\n\nI'm checking the timestamp 1744944660000 — that converts to 1744944660 seconds, which is far too large to be a recent Unix timestamp.\n\nLet me recalculate: if 1744937460000 is in milliseconds, dividing by 1000 gives 1744937460 seconds, which converts to approximately April 17, 2026 around 2:11 PM UTC or 10:11 PM Beijing time. This suggests the heartbeat timestamps are actually in seconds, not milliseconds as I initially thought.\n\nConverting 1744937460 to a human-readable date: 1744937460 seconds from the epoch. I should probably stick with milliseconds since that's the safer assumption for JavaScript timestamps.\n\nActually, the cron payload shows the current time as Friday, April 17th, 2026 at 10:11 PM UTC, which converts to 2026-04-17T14:11:00Z in UTC. If the last heartbeat was recorded at 2026-04-17T21:11:00Z, that would be 7 hours earlier, but I need to figure out if those timestamps are in seconds or milliseconds.\n\nLooking at the heartbeat file more carefully, 1744930260000 as a Unix timestamp in seconds converts to approximately May 2025, which doesn't align with the stated date of April 2026. I should reconsider whether these timestamps are in milliseconds instead.\n\nIf I divide by 1000 to convert from milliseconds, I get 1744930260 seconds, which still gives May 2025 when converted. This suggests the timestamp format might be inconsistent with the date it claims to represent, or there's a mismatch in how the timestamps are being stored versus how they're being interpreted. I need to figure out the correct timestamp for 2026-04-17T22:11:00Z. Since 1744930260000 ms corresponds to 2026-04-17T21:11:00Z, adding 3600 seconds (1 hour) gives me 1744933860000 ms for 2026-04-17T22:11:00Z. Now I can set up the operations: updating the heartbeat timestamp to 1744933860000, creating a new task record, and adding an entry to the progress table."},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me update the heartbeat and create a new check record:\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_fu7r3urqsxum_1","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"memory/heartbeat-state.json","content":"{\n  \"last_heartbeat\": \"2026-04-17T22:11:00Z\",\n  \"instance\": \"小柱\",\n  \"status\": \"活跃\",\n  \"auto_check_job_id\": \"53656e40-4892-4759-a73e-a11dc01082b5\"\n}\n"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_fu7r3urqsxum_